course: dictionary
lesson: 7782
title: Looks can trick you
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things that look like something else. Pyrite is a mineral, a natural substance found inside rocks. «That is how they catch fool's gold, pyrite, which glitters like gold» tells you that pyrite shines like real gold. The word fool's gold is a clue too: a fool is someone who is tricked, so fool's gold is something people wrongly think is gold.
explain: The sentence gives more clues: pyrite is harder and much lighter than gold, and it leaves a black streak. Real gold is heavier, so a pale, light stone that looks golden is probably not gold. Ask yourself: what would real gold be like? Then compare.
explain: Check your meaning by putting it into the sentence. «Pyrite is a shiny, pale gold-coloured mineral» still makes sense, so you know you have it right.
fact: Pyrite is a shiny, pale gold-coloured mineral made of iron and sulphur.
fact: Pyrite is called fool's gold because people mistook it for real gold.
fact: Pyrite is harder and more brittle than gold, and it often forms cubes.
fact: Gold rush miners were fooled by pyrite, because it looked like the gold they wanted.
fact: Pyrite is an uncountable noun: you say some pyrite, or a pyrite crystal.
exercise: What is pyrite? | *A shiny, pale gold-coloured mineral made of iron and sulphur | A soft metal used for making jewellery | A brown wooden tool for digging | A grey, wet type of weather
exercise: Why is pyrite called fool's gold? | Because it can be eaten | Because it is made from fools | *Because people mistook it for real gold | Because it is the most expensive gold in the world
exercise: «...which glitters like gold» Find and copy the word that means shines brightly. | *glitters | harder | catch | streak
test: Which clue in the sentence shows pyrite is not real gold? | It glitters like gold | *It is much lighter than gold | It catches people out | It is a mineral
test: In science lessons, what shape does pyrite often form? | Ribbons | *Cubes | Circles | Stars
exam: What does the phrase fool's gold mean? | A gold coin from a rich man's purse | *Something that looks like gold but is not real gold | Gold that a foolish man has lost | A golden crown worn by a king
